  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Meaning of Time Invariance",
    "lesson_core_move": "Recognize time invariance by interpreting whether delaying or advancing any continuous-time input causes the system output to undergo the identical delay or advance without changing shape or rule.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Meaning of Time Invariance",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Meaning of Time Invariance",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A continuous-time system is time invariant when shifting the input by $T$ before the system produces the same shift by $T$ at the output after the system. In other words, the system commutes with input shifts: the shape and rule of the output stay the same, and only the time origin moves.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_1(t)=x(t-T), \\; y_{tts}(t)=S\\{x_1(t)\\}=S\\{x(t-T)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To test the other path, start with the system output $y(t)=S\\{x(t)\\}$ and then shift that output by the same amount. The result is the system-then-shift path, abbreviated $y_{sts}(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_{sts}(t)=y(t-T), \\; \\text{compare } y_{tts}(t) \\stackrel{?}{=} y_{sts}(t) \\text{ for all } t,T",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The test is simple: a system is time invariant if the two paths are identical for every input $x(t)$ and every shift $T$. If the shift direction, the shift size, or the output rule changes, the system is not time invariant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cleanest base case is the memoryless system $y(t)=x(t)$. For a delay of $T$, both paths give $x(t-T)$, so this is the simplest template for the later matching-delay, matching-advance, and mismatch examples.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Meaning of Time Invariance",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing a Matching Output Delay",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Test time invariance for $y(t)=2x(t-1)$ under input delay $x_1(t)=x(t-T)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is an input delay $x_1(t)=x(t-T)$. To test time invariance, keep the system rule fixed as $y(t)=2x(t-1)$ and compare the time-shift-then-system path with the system-then-time-shift path.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_{tts}(t)=2x((t)-1-T)=2x(t-T-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now compute the system-then-shift path from the unshifted output. The internal delay of $1$ stays inside the rule, and the output is shifted by the same amount $T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_{sts}(t)=2x((t-T)-1)=2x(t-T-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two expressions are identical term by term: both paths give $2x(t-T-1)$. That means the input delay propagates through the system unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So this system is time invariant for this delay test. The fixed offset inside $x(t-1)$ does not break shift commutation because both paths still produce the same delayed output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Meaning of Time Invariance",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Recognizing a Matching Output Advance",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Test time invariance for $y(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)$ using an advance input $x_1(t)=x(t+T)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is an advance input $x_1(t)=x(t+T)$. Keep the system rule fixed as $y(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)$ and compare the time-shift-then-system path with the system-then-time-shift path.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_{tts}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t+T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now compute the system-then-shift path from the unshifted output. The output is first $y(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)$, and then it is advanced by the same amount $T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_{sts}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t+T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_{tts}(t)=y_{sts}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t+T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two paths match exactly, so this is a matched output advance. That means the system is time invariant here as well, and the sign change from delay to advance does not change the compare-step method.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Meaning of Time Invariance",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Recognizing When a Shift Changes the Output Pattern",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Check whether $y(t)=t\\,x(t)$ is time invariant under input shift $x_1(t)=x(t-T)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is an input shift $x_1(t)=x(t-T)$. Keep the system rule fixed as $y(t)=t\\,x(t)$ and compare the time-shift-then-system path with the system-then-time-shift path.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_{tts}(t)=t\\,x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now compute the system-then-shift path from the unshifted output. First the system gives $y(t)=t\\,x(t)$, and then the output is delayed by the same amount $T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_{sts}(t)=(t-T)x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_{tts}(t)=t\\,x(t-T), \\; y_{sts}(t)=(t-T)x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two paths do not match because the factor of $t$ becomes $t-T$ after the output shift. That means the output pattern changes with the shift, so this system is not time invariant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "question_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Meaning of Time Invariance",
    "lesson_core_move": "Recognize time invariance by interpreting whether delaying or advancing any continuous-time input causes the system output to undergo the identical delay or advance without changing shape or rule.",
    "section_count": 3,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Meaning of Time Invariance",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing a Matching Output Delay",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $y(t)=2x(t-1)$ and delay $x_1(t)=x(t-T)$, which pair is the correct time-shift-then-system and system-then-shift comparison?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=2x((t)-1-T)=2x(t-T-1)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=2x((t-T)-1)=2x(t-T-1)$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=2x((t)-1+T)=2x(t+T-1)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=2x((t+T)-1)=2x(t+T-1)$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=2x(t-1)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=2x((t-T)-1)$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=2x(t-T-1)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=2x((t+T)-1)=2x(t+T-1)$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=2x((t)-T)-1$ and $y_{sts}(t)=2x((t-T)-1)$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "A",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For TTS, replace $x(t)$ with $x(t-T)$ inside $y(t)=2x(t-1)$ to get $2x((t)-1-T)$. For STS, form $y_{sts}(t)=y(t-T)=2x((t-T)-1)$. These are identical, so the delayed output matches exactly.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "question_index": 2,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $y(t)=5x(t+1)$ with input shift $x_1(t)=x(t-2)$, which option correctly shows the two paths and the match status?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=5x((t)+1-2)=5x(t-1)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=5x((t-2)+1)=5x(t-1)$. The paths match.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=5x((t+2)-1)=5x(t+1)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=5x((t)-2+1)=5x(t-1)$, so they do not match.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=5x(t+1-2)=5x(t-1)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=5x((t)-1)=5x(t-1)$. These differ only by the symbol used for the input shift.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=5x(t+1)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=5x(t-1)$, so the system does not preserve the delayed input.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=2x((t)+1-2)=2x(t-1)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=5x((t-2)+1)=5x(t-1)$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "A",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Use the same compare workflow with fixed gain $5$: $y_{tts}(t)=5x(t+1-2)=5x(t-1)$ from substitution into $x(t-2)$, and $y_{sts}(t)=y(t-2)=5x((t-2)+1)=5x(t-1)$. Both paths are identical, so this is still a matching-output-delay case.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Meaning of Time Invariance",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Recognizing a Matching Output Advance",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $y(t)=3x(t)$ and advanced input $x_1(t)=x(t+T)$, which pair of path outputs is correct?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=3x(t+T)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=3x(t+T)$, so the two paths match exactly.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=3x(t+T)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=3x(t-T)$, which swaps the shift sign in the second path.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=3x(t-T)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=3x(t+T)$, which flips the sign in the first path only.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=3x((t+T)-1)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=3x(t+T)$, which is applying the system delay in the wrong place.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_{tts}(t)=3x(t+T)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=3x(t+T)+1$, which adds a constant not present in the system rule.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "$y_{tts}(t)=3x(t+T)$ and $y_{sts}(t)=3x(t+T)$.",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For TTS, substitute the shifted input into the rule: $y_{tts}(t)=3x_1(t)=3x(t+T)$. For STS, shift the unshifted output by $T$: $y(t)=3x(t) \\implies y_{sts}(t)=y(t+T)=3x(t+T)$. They match term by term.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "question_index": 2,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $y(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)$ and $x_1(t)=x(t+2T)$, which pair best shows the matched output advance?",
                "latex": "",
